  "narrative_body": "The American president publicly confirmed this week that Saudi Arabia had talked him out of striking Iran — naming Mohammed bin Salman and the Gulf monarchies as the leaders who asked him to hold off — a moment that converted years of behind-the-scenes diplomatic maneuvering into on-the-record presidential testimony.\n\nThe American president described shelving \"a very major attack\" on Iran after Gulf allies asked \"if we could put it off for two or three days.\" Faisal bin Farhan, the foreign minister, welcomed the pause, urged Tehran to seize the opening, and stated Saudi Arabia was \"awaiting Iran's response.\" Mr bin Farhan also ran an intensive consultation circuit — calls with counterparts in Egypt, Kuwait, Qatar, Syria, Iran, Portugal, and Nepal — building a coalition of voices behind Saudi-led de-escalation. The Syria call was the most structurally telling: Saudi normalization of the post-Assad transitional government is advancing quietly, extending Riyadh's influence into territory Iran once dominated. The week also produced a formal multilateral deliverable: the GCC-UK free trade agreement, signed in London by GCC Secretary-General Jassim Al-Budaiwi and UK Trade Minister Chris Bryant after four years of negotiations, covering $53 billion in annual bilateral trade across goods, services, digital trade, and financial services.\n\nThe diplomatic posture rests on a military architecture being assembled in parallel. Reuters confirmed, citing five independent officials, that Pakistan has deployed 8,000 troops, a squadron of roughly 16 JF-17 fighters, two drone squadrons, and a Chinese HQ-9 air defense system to Saudi Arabia under a confidential mutual defense pact signed in September 2025. Saudi Arabia finances the deployment; Pakistan operates the equipment. The pact provides for up to 80,000 Pakistani troops if needed. Pakistan's defense minister implied it \"encompasses all military means\" — language pointing toward a nuclear umbrella without explicitly confirming one. Saudi Arabia is now the first Arab Gulf state operating under a formal mutual defense arrangement with a nuclear-armed power. The deployment fills gaps Saudi Arabia cannot close on its own: Chinese-origin air defense, jointly manufactured fighter aircraft, and an extended deterrent the US-Saudi relationship has never formally provided.\n\nThat the deployment is needed is not in doubt. On May 17, Saudi air defenses intercepted and destroyed three drones entering from Iraqi airspace. The Ministry of Defense warned it reserves \"the full right to respond at the appropriate time and place.\" Iraq denied knowledge of launches from its territory. Reporting by Al-Monitor and the Wall Street Journal, cited in analysis by the Foundation for Defense of Democracies, confirmed that Iranian-backed Shia militias based in Iraq are behind the bulk of attacks on Saudi Arabia and Kuwait, and that both countries have conducted multiple counter-strikes inside Iraq. A separate drone attack on the UAE's Barakah nuclear plant on May 19 was also traced to Iraq. Saudi Arabia is simultaneously the public de-escalation broker and an active belligerent — a posture it sustains by keeping one track entirely visible and the other entirely covert.\n\nEconomically, the week produced a cluster of signals that together describe a country under significant fiscal strain. NEOM formally halted The Line until at least 2030, postponed the Trojena ski resort — scheduled to host the 2029 Asian Winter Games — and cancelled the €1.4 billion Webuild high-speed rail contract, the company's second NEOM cancellation in two months. The Line's population target has collapsed from 9 million to 100,000. Finance Minister Mohammed al-Jadaan's gloss on the reversals — \"if we announce something and we need to adjust it... or cancel it, we will without blinking\" — was the highest-level acknowledgment yet that megaproject recalibration is deliberate policy. NEOM will redirect roughly $3 billion to developing Oxagon as a port and data center hub: the extractable value from a Red Sea coastal position once the fantastical elements are stripped away.\n\nThe fiscal pressure shows up in the numbers. JODI data confirmed Saudi crude exports fell to 4.974 million barrels per day in March — the lowest since the data series began in January 2002 — even as the East-West pipeline ran at its 7 million barrel-per-day maximum. Aramco produced 7.76 million barrels a day in March; the gap represents barrels stranded by the Hormuz closure, with no pipeline fix available at that volume. Saudi Arabia also ramped fuel oil imports 86% year-on-year in April as gas-fired power generation declined, adding import costs against higher revenues. Meanwhile, the Public Investment Fund raised $7 billion in its largest-ever single bond sale, drawing $23.8 billion in orders across three tranches. The 30-year tranche priced 40 basis points wider than PIF's pre-war January 2025 benchmark — the war premium is built into the yield curve and has not moved. PIF's cash reserves sit at roughly $15 billion, a six-year low. Its US equity portfolio has fallen from a peak of $25.6 billion to $12 billion as the fund formalizes an 80% domestic, 20% international deployment strategy.\n\nOne clarifying signal on OPEC+ came not from Riyadh but from Abu Dhabi. UAE diplomatic adviser Anwar Gargash confirmed that the Emirates' OPEC withdrawal was the product of a three-year strategic study — a decision to maximize revenues before the energy transition, framed as entering the \"autumn of the hydrocarbon age.\" The departure predated the Iran conflict and, on that timeline, is not reversible under different regional conditions. Saudi Arabia's OPEC+ leadership now rests on a more compact grouping — Iraq, Kuwait, Algeria, Kazakhstan, Russia, Oman — with fewer internal incentives for the quota-busting that defined the UAE's final years in the organisation.\n\nAgainst this backdrop, Aramco and the French firm Pasqal inaugurated Saudi Arabia's first quantum computer — a 200-qubit system at Aramco's Dhahran data center, offered to enterprises and universities as a cloud service. It is a modest but concrete step in the technology localization that now constitutes Vision 2030's operational core: Oxagon for port and data infrastructure, quantum computing at Dhahran, airport expansion in Riyadh — the residue of ambition after fiscal reality has done its filtering.",
